<section class="home-map"><div><p class="eyebrow">Gallery map</p><h2>Four ways into the craft</h2><p>Start with the part of watchmaking that already catches your eye. Each route builds practical understanding without turning appreciation into a test.</p></div><ol><li><b>01</b><h3>Read the dial</h3><p>See how hierarchy, contrast and negative space organise information.</p></li><li><b>02</b><h3>Study the case</h3><p>Notice proportion, profile, finishing and how a watch meets the wrist.</p></li><li><b>03</b><h3>Follow the movement</h3><p>Trace stored energy through wheels, escapement and regulating organ.</p></li><li><b>04</b><h3>Build a ritual</h3><p>Choose, wear and care for watches with calm, repeatable habits.</p></li></ol></section>
<section class="feature-window"><img class="" src="assets/images/workbench.jpg" alt="Mechanical watch components and precision tools on a watchmaker workbench" loading="lazy"><div><p class="eyebrow">On the workbench</p><h2>A movement is a conversation between forces.</h2><p>The mainspring stores energy, the gear train carries it, and the escapement releases it in controlled steps. Understanding that conversation makes the smallest components feel legible.</p><a class="btn pale" href="inside-a-mechanical-movement.html">Open the movement guide</a></div></section>
